"45b_minesweeper.mst — Minesweeper in the native Metal game pane
 (docs/gamepane_design.md). Launch it from the Demos menu, or a Workspace:

     Minesweeper launch
     Minesweeper launchWithSeed: 12345    'the same board every time'

 LEFT click reveals a square, RIGHT click plants or lifts a flag, SPACE deals a
 new board and Escape quits. An 18x12 field with 32 mines, at 16 pixels a
 square.

 This is the demo the pane's two mouse buttons were made for, and it reads the
 pointer the OTHER way round from Life. Life wants the button's LEVEL — hold
 and drag and it paints a stroke of cells. Minesweeper wants the EDGE: one
 click must reveal exactly one square, so it compares each button against what
 it saw last frame and acts only on the frame the button goes DOWN. Both
 patterns come off the same GameFrame; which one a game wants is a question
 about the game, not about the engine.

 THE FIRST CLICK IS ALWAYS SAFE. The mines are not dealt until you have
 clicked, and then they are dealt around that square and its eight neighbours —
 so the first click always opens into empty space rather than ending the game
 on move one. That is what real Minesweeper does, and a demo that can kill you
 instantly is a bad advertisement for anything.

 Hit a mine and the screen SHAKES. The board is 288x192 inside a 320x240 pane,
 so there is a margin all round it doing nothing — the shake happens into that
 overscan, which is why the whole field can lurch about for half a second
 without a square sliding off the edge or the picture needing to be scaled.
 The bang, the clicks and the flags all make a noise (Sound explode/click/blip,
 and powerup when the field is cleared).

 Drawing: the board is composed into one reused ByteArray of palette indices
 and handed over in a single `blit:`, recomposed only when something changed —
 which for Minesweeper is 'only when you click', so a board just sitting there
 costs nothing. The mine, the flag AND the counts are all pixel art stamped
 into that buffer (mineArt/flagArt/digitArt), in the colours the original used:
 1 blue, 2 green, 3 red, 4 navy and so on. They live in the picture rather than
 in a text layer for a specific reason — the explosion shakes the board by
 SCROLLING it, and a fixed text layer would leave every number nailed to the
 glass while its square slid out from under it.

 The HUD is the opposite case and lives in the TEXT PLANE
 (docs/shared_screen_memory_design.md SM1): a grid of cells in shared memory
 that the VM writes with stores. It is deliberately viewport-fixed, so the
 readout holds still while the field lurches, and it is rewritten from scratch
 every single frame because that now costs nothing to send. It did not always:
 this demo's first version redrew a command-based overlay every frame, which
 re-sent one message per revealed number sixty times a second. That bug is the
 reason the text plane exists, and it cannot recur — a store is not a message.

 In a headless VM every game primitive silently fails, so loading this class
 outside the GUI is harmless — which is how its regression test drives it."
"I am Minesweeper, played in the native game pane: an 18x12 field of 32 mines.
 Launch me from the Demos menu, or a Workspace: Minesweeper launch (or
 Minesweeper launchWithSeed: 12345 for a board you can replay). LEFT click
 reveals a square, RIGHT click flags one, SPACE deals a new board, Escape
 quits. My first click is always safe — I deal the mines around wherever you
 clicked, so the opening move always opens a space. Revealing an empty square
 floods outward to its neighbours the way the original does. I read the mouse
 buttons on their EDGE (one click, one square), which is the opposite of Life's
 level-triggered painting, and both come off the same GameFrame. Harmless to
 load in a headless VM (every game primitive silently fails there)."
Object subclass: Minesweeper [
    | pane cols rows cell x0 y0 mines state counts buf
      dealt over won flags revealed rng ticks startTick endTick
      prevLeft prevRight prevSpace dirty shake margin worldW worldH |
    <classVars: Current>

    "Cell state — `state` holds one of these per square."
    Minesweeper class >> hidden   [ ^0 ]
    Minesweeper class >> shown    [ ^1 ]
    Minesweeper class >> flagged  [ ^2 ]

    "Palette slots. The original's colours, loaded over the default set so the
     board looks like the thing it is a port of rather than like whatever
     happened to be in the palette."
    Minesweeper class >> cFace    [ ^16 ]   "192,192,192 — the square's face"
    Minesweeper class >> cLight   [ ^17 ]   "255,255,255 — raised bevel"
    Minesweeper class >> cDark    [ ^18 ]   "128,128,128 — sunken bevel, grid"
    Minesweeper class >> cBlack   [ ^19 ]   "the mine, the flag's pole"
    Minesweeper class >> cRed     [ ^20 ]   "the flag, and the mine you hit"
    Minesweeper class >> cWhite   [ ^21 ]   "the mine's highlight"
    "22..29 are the counts 1..8, in the colours the original used. They are
     palette slots rather than RGB because the numbers are drawn INTO the
     scrolling buffer with everything else — see drawNumber:atX:y:."
    Minesweeper class >> cNumber  [ ^21 ]   "count n uses cNumber + n"

    Minesweeper class >> launch [
        "Deal a fresh board from the wall clock, so no two games are alike."
        ^self launchWithSeed: (Time millisecondClockValue \\ 2147483646) + 1
    ]

    Minesweeper class >> launchWithSeed: aSeed [
        "Deal a board from a known seed — the same seed always deals the same
         mines, which is what makes this game testable and what lets you replay
         one you liked."
        ^self new startWithSeed: aSeed
    ]

    Minesweeper class >> current [
        "The game most recently launched, so a Workspace can question it while
         it runs — `Minesweeper current minesLeft` — and so the regression test
         can reach the board."
        ^Current
    ]

    startWithSeed: aSeed [
        Current := self.
        pane := GamePane new.
        cols := 18.
        rows := 12.
        cell := 16.
        "Ask for a world 16 pixels bigger than the viewport on every side. That
         border is what the explosion shakes INTO: scrolling the view across it
         costs one command a frame and redraws nothing, where moving the board
         by redrawing it would rasterize the whole picture sixty times a second
         for no reason. Send it before drawing anything — it decides how the
         pane is built."
        margin := 16.
        pane overscan: margin.
        worldW := 320 + (margin * 2).
        worldH := 240 + (margin * 2).
        "Drawing is now in WORLD coordinates, so everything shifts by the
         margin: at rest the view sits at (margin, margin) and what used to be
         pane (0,0) is world (16,16)."
        x0 := margin + ((320 - (cols * cell)) // 2).
        y0 := margin + 30.
        buf := ByteArray new: worldW * worldH.
        self buildPalette.
        self dealWithSeed: aSeed.
        pane onStep: [ :frame | self stepWith: frame ]; run.
        ^self
    ]

    dealWithSeed: aSeed [
        "A new board: everything hidden, no mines placed yet. The mines wait
         for the first click (see plantAround:y:) so that click can be safe."
        rng := Random newWithSeed: aSeed.
        mines := ByteArray new: cols * rows.
        state := ByteArray new: cols * rows.
        counts := ByteArray new: cols * rows.
        dealt := false.
        over := false.
        won := false.
        flags := 0.
        revealed := 0.
        ticks := 0.
        startTick := 0.
        endTick := 0.
        prevLeft := false.
        prevRight := false.
        prevSpace := false.
        dirty := true.
        shake := 0.
        "A new board always looks straight ahead — a deal arriving mid-shake
         must not leave the view parked off-centre."
        pane scrollTo: margin y: margin.
        ^self
    ]

    buildPalette [
        "The original's grey face, its white and dark-grey bevels, and the
         three colours the mine and flag are drawn from."
        pane paletteAt: Minesweeper cFace  r: 192 g: 192 b: 192.
        pane paletteAt: Minesweeper cLight r: 255 g: 255 b: 255.
        pane paletteAt: Minesweeper cDark  r: 128 g: 128 b: 128.
        pane paletteAt: Minesweeper cBlack r: 0   g: 0   b: 0.
        pane paletteAt: Minesweeper cRed   r: 220 g: 40  b: 40.
        pane paletteAt: Minesweeper cWhite r: 255 g: 255 b: 255.
        "The counts, 1..8 — blue, green, red, navy, maroon, teal, black, grey."
        pane paletteAt: 22 r: 40  g: 80  b: 255.
        pane paletteAt: 23 r: 30  g: 150 b: 40.
        pane paletteAt: 24 r: 235 g: 50  b: 50.
        pane paletteAt: 25 r: 30  g: 30  b: 160.
        pane paletteAt: 26 r: 150 g: 30  b: 30.
        pane paletteAt: 27 r: 20  g: 150 b: 150.
        pane paletteAt: 28 r: 20  g: 20  b: 20.
        pane paletteAt: 29 r: 90  g: 90  b: 90.
        ^self
    ]

    "--- the field ---"

    indexX: x y: y [ ^(y * cols) + x + 1 ]

    onBoardX: x y: y [ ^x >= 0 and: [ x < cols and: [ y >= 0 and: [ y < rows ] ] ] ]

    mineAtX: x y: y [ ^(mines at: (self indexX: x y: y)) = 1 ]

    stateAtX: x y: y [ ^state at: (self indexX: x y: y) ]

    countAtX: x y: y [ ^counts at: (self indexX: x y: y) ]

    minesLeft [
        "What the counter shows: total mines less flags planted. It can go
         negative if you over-flag, exactly as the original's does."
        ^32 - flags
    ]

    isOver [ ^over ]
    hasWon [ ^won ]
    isDealt [ ^dealt ]
    revealedCount [ "How many squares have been opened so far." ^revealed ]

    plantAround: sx y: sy [
        "Deal 32 mines, refusing the clicked square and its eight neighbours so
         the opening click always lands in open space. Called once, on the
         first reveal."
        | placed x y i |
        placed := 0.
        [ placed < 32 ] whileTrue: [
            x := (rng next * cols) truncated min: cols - 1.
            y := (rng next * rows) truncated min: rows - 1.
            i := self indexX: x y: y.
            "Not already a mine, and not in the safe 3x3 around the first click."
            ((mines at: i) = 0 and: [ ((x - sx) abs > 1) or: [ (y - sy) abs > 1 ] ])
                ifTrue: [ mines at: i put: 1. placed := placed + 1 ] ].
        self countNeighbours.
        dealt := true.
        startTick := ticks.
        ^self
    ]

    countNeighbours [
        "Fill in each square's neighbouring-mine count, once, when the mines
         are dealt — the numbers never change after that."
        | n |
        0 to: rows - 1 do: [ :y |
            0 to: cols - 1 do: [ :x |
                n := 0.
                -1 to: 1 do: [ :dy |
                    -1 to: 1 do: [ :dx |
                        ((dx = 0 and: [ dy = 0 ]) not
                            and: [ (self onBoardX: x + dx y: y + dy)
                                and: [ self mineAtX: x + dx y: y + dy ] ])
                                    ifTrue: [ n := n + 1 ] ] ].
                counts at: (self indexX: x y: y) put: n ] ].
        ^self
    ]

    "--- playing ---"

    revealX: x y: y [
        "Reveal one square. A mine ends the game; an empty square (no
         neighbouring mines) floods outward to its neighbours. The flood is an
         explicit worklist rather than recursion — a big empty region would
         otherwise recurse as deep as the board is large."
        | work px py i |
        (self onBoardX: x y: y) ifFalse: [ ^self ].
        dealt ifFalse: [ self plantAround: x y: y ].
        (self stateAtX: x y: y) = Minesweeper hidden ifFalse: [ ^self ].
        (self mineAtX: x y: y) ifTrue: [ ^self loseAtX: x y: y ].
        work := OrderedCollection new.
        work add: (Array with: x with: y).
        [ work isEmpty ] whileFalse: [
            | pair |
            pair := work removeFirst.
            px := pair at: 1.
            py := pair at: 2.
            i := self indexX: px y: py.
            (state at: i) = Minesweeper hidden ifTrue: [
                state at: i put: Minesweeper shown.
                revealed := revealed + 1.
                "Only an empty square spreads; a numbered one is a wall."
                (counts at: i) = 0 ifTrue: [
                    -1 to: 1 do: [ :dy |
                        -1 to: 1 do: [ :dx |
                            ((self onBoardX: px + dx y: py + dy)
                                and: [ (self stateAtX: px + dx y: py + dy)
                                        = Minesweeper hidden ])
                                ifTrue: [ work add: (Array with: px + dx with: py + dy) ] ] ] ] ] ].
        "One click, one sound — this is reached only when a square actually
         opened, so a click on empty space or an already-open square stays
         silent rather than chattering. The mine case never gets here; it
         returned above with the explosion instead."
        Sound click play.
        dirty := true.
        self checkWon.
        ^self
    ]

    flagX: x y: y [
        "Plant or lift a flag. A revealed square cannot be flagged — there is
         nothing left to be unsure about."
        | i s |
        (self onBoardX: x y: y) ifFalse: [ ^self ].
        i := self indexX: x y: y.
        s := state at: i.
        s = Minesweeper shown ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        s = Minesweeper flagged
            ifTrue: [ state at: i put: Minesweeper hidden. flags := flags - 1 ]
            ifFalse: [ state at: i put: Minesweeper flagged. flags := flags + 1 ].
        Sound blip play.
        dirty := true.
        ^self
    ]

    loseAtX: x y: y [
        "You hit one. Show every mine, and remember which one it was so it can
         be drawn on red."
        state at: (self indexX: x y: y) put: Minesweeper shown.
        over := true.
        won := false.
        endTick := ticks.
        "Half a second of rattle, and the bang. Both are no-ops in a headless
         VM, so the regression tests never hear a thing."
        shake := 30.
        Sound explode play.
        dirty := true.
        ^self
    ]

    checkWon [
        "Won when every square that is NOT a mine has been revealed — flags do
         not enter into it, exactly as in the original."
        revealed = ((cols * rows) - 32) ifTrue: [
            over := true.
            won := true.
            endTick := ticks.
            Sound powerup play ].
        ^self
    ]

    elapsedSeconds [
        "Seconds since the first click, frozen when the game ends. Counted in
         frames rather than read off the clock, so it cannot disagree with what
         is on screen."
        | stop |
        dealt ifFalse: [ ^0 ].
        stop := over ifTrue: [ endTick ] ifFalse: [ ticks ].
        ^(stop - startTick) // 60
    ]

    "--- input ---"

    "The pointer arrives in VIEWPORT pixels while the board is drawn in WORLD
     pixels, so the hit test has to put the scroll back: world = viewport +
     scroll, and the view sits at (margin, margin) whenever the board is
     playable. It is only ever elsewhere during the explosion shake, and clicks
     are ignored once the game is over — so this is exact, not approximate."
    cellXFor: px [ ^(px + margin - x0) // cell ]
    cellYFor: py [ ^(py + margin - y0) // cell ]

    readMouseFrom: frame [
        "EDGE-triggered, unlike Life: act on the frame a button goes down, not
         while it is held. Held would reveal a square every frame the button
         was down, which is a click that eats the whole board."
        | left right cx cy |
        left := frame mouseDown.
        right := frame mouseRightDown.
        (frame mouseInPane and: [ over not ]) ifTrue: [
            cx := self cellXFor: frame mouseX.
            cy := self cellYFor: frame mouseY.
            (left and: [ prevLeft not ]) ifTrue: [ self revealX: cx y: cy ].
            (right and: [ prevRight not ]) ifTrue: [ self flagX: cx y: cy ] ].
        prevLeft := left.
        prevRight := right.
        ^self
    ]

    readSpaceFrom: frame [
        "SPACE deals a new board — on the edge, again, or holding it would
         reshuffle 60 times a second."
        | down |
        down := frame keyHeld: GamePane keyA.
        (down and: [ prevSpace not ]) ifTrue: [
            | seed |
            seed := (Time millisecondClockValue \\ 2147483646) + 1.
            self dealWithSeed: seed.
            prevSpace := true.
            ^self ].
        prevSpace := down.
        ^self
    ]

    "--- the frame ---"

    stepWith: frame [
        "One frame: read the input, redraw if anything changed, and present.
         `present` runs every frame — it is the one moment the GUI shows the
         pane — but the redraw above it does NOT, and that distinction is
         load-bearing here. The text overlay is RETAINED, so redrawing it every
         frame re-sent a message per revealed number, sixty times a second:
         about six thousand commands a second down the VM→GUI channel for a
         board that had not changed. Now the board redraws only when something
         actually happens — a click, the clock ticking over, or the explosion
         shaking the screen."
        ticks := ticks + 1.
        self readSpaceFrom: frame.
        self readMouseFrom: frame.
        self advanceShake.
        "The BOARD is repainted only when it changed — that is a blit, and a
         blit is a real command carrying a real frame. The HUD is drawn every
         single frame regardless, because since SM1 it is stores into the text
         plane and costs nothing to send.

         Separating the two is what makes an idle board completely silent. The
         clock ticking over used to dirty the whole frame once a second purely
         so the seconds would update; now the seconds live somewhere that is
         free to rewrite, and a board nobody is touching emits exactly one
         command per frame: the present that shows it."
        dirty ifTrue: [ self paint. dirty := false ].
        self drawHud.
        pane present.
        ^self
    ]

    advanceShake [
        "The explosion's screen shake, spent one frame at a time. The board is
         288x192 inside a 320x240 pane, so there is a margin all round it doing
         nothing — the shake happens INTO that overscan, which is why the board
         can lurch about without a single square sliding off the edge or the
         picture having to be scaled.

         The offset alternates every frame on x and every other frame on y, so
         it reads as a violent rattle rather than a smooth slide, and the
         amplitude decays with the remaining frames so it settles rather than
         stopping dead."
        | amp dx dy |
        shake = 0 ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        shake := shake - 1.
        amp := (shake * 12) // 30.
        dx := (shake \\ 2) = 0 ifTrue: [ amp ] ifFalse: [ 0 - amp ].
        dy := (shake \\ 4) < 2 ifTrue: [ amp ] ifFalse: [ 0 - amp ].
        "One command, no redraw: `dirty` is deliberately NOT set. The board is
         already drawn; all that moves is where the viewport looks."
        pane scrollTo: margin + dx y: margin + dy.
        ^self
    ]

    "--- drawing ---"

    fill: px y: py width: w height: h color: c [
        "Fill a rectangle of WORLD pixels straight into the frame buffer."
        | idx |
        0 to: h - 1 do: [ :dy |
            idx := ((py + dy) * worldW) + px + 1.
            0 to: w - 1 do: [ :dx | buf at: idx + dx put: c ] ].
        ^self
    ]

    paint [
        "Compose the whole world: background, then every square, then blit it
         in one command. The background covers the overscan border too, so
         whatever the shake scrolls into view is the same grey as the rest and
         not uninitialised memory."
        | s |
        self fill: 0 y: 0 width: worldW height: worldH color: Minesweeper cDark.
        0 to: rows - 1 do: [ :y |
            0 to: cols - 1 do: [ :x |
                s := self stateAtX: x y: y.
                s = Minesweeper shown
                    ifTrue: [ self paintShownX: x y: y ]
                    ifFalse: [ self paintHiddenX: x y: y flagged: s = Minesweeper flagged ] ] ].
        pane blit: buf.
        ^self
    ]

    paintHiddenX: x y: y flagged: isFlagged [
        "An unclicked square: the raised look — face, white along the top and
         left, dark grey along the bottom and right. Two pixels of bevel is
         what reads as 'button' at this size."
        | px py |
        px := x0 + (x * cell).
        py := y0 + (y * cell).
        self fill: px y: py width: cell height: cell color: Minesweeper cFace.
        self fill: px y: py width: cell height: 2 color: Minesweeper cLight.
        self fill: px y: py width: 2 height: cell color: Minesweeper cLight.
        self fill: px y: py + cell - 2 width: cell height: 2 color: Minesweeper cDark.
        self fill: px + cell - 2 y: py width: 2 height: cell color: Minesweeper cDark.
        "When the game is lost, every mine shows — even under a flag, which is
         how you find out the flag was wrong."
        (over and: [ self mineAtX: x y: y ])
            ifTrue: [ ^self stamp: self mineArt at: px + 2 y: py + 2 ].
        isFlagged ifTrue: [ self stamp: self flagArt at: px + 2 y: py + 2 ].
        ^self
    ]

    paintShownX: x y: y [
        "A revealed square: flat, with one dark pixel along the top and left as
         the grid line. A revealed mine is the one you hit — it gets a red
         ground so it is obvious which move ended it."
        | px py |
        px := x0 + (x * cell).
        py := y0 + (y * cell).
        (self mineAtX: x y: y)
            ifTrue: [ self fill: px y: py width: cell height: cell color: Minesweeper cRed ]
            ifFalse: [ self fill: px y: py width: cell height: cell color: Minesweeper cFace ].
        self fill: px y: py width: cell height: 1 color: Minesweeper cDark.
        self fill: px y: py width: 1 height: cell color: Minesweeper cDark.
        (self mineAtX: x y: y)
            ifTrue: [ self stamp: self mineArt at: px + 2 y: py + 2 ]
            ifFalse: [
                (self countAtX: x y: y) > 0
                    ifTrue: [ self drawNumber: (self countAtX: x y: y) atX: x y: y ] ].
        ^self
    ]

    stamp: art at: px y: py [
        "Draw pixel art — an Array of equal-length row Strings — into the frame
         buffer. `.` is transparent, so the art keeps the square underneath it;
         every other character names a colour through colourFor:."
        | row c |
        1 to: art size do: [ :r |
            row := art at: r.
            1 to: row size do: [ :cIdx |
                c := self colourFor: (row at: cIdx).
                c = 0 ifFalse: [
                    buf at: ((py + r - 1) * worldW) + px + cIdx put: c ] ] ].
        ^self
    ]

    colourFor: aChar [
        "Art characters to palette slots; 0 means leave the pixel alone.

         `=`, NOT `==`. String>>at: hands back a fresh Character rather than the
         canonical one out of Character's table, so identity is false even for
         the same character and every piece of art silently drew nothing —
         no mine, no flag, no number, and no error to say so."
        aChar = $K ifTrue: [ ^Minesweeper cBlack ].
        aChar = $W ifTrue: [ ^Minesweeper cWhite ].
        aChar = $R ifTrue: [ ^Minesweeper cRed ].
        ^0
    ]

    mineArt [
        "The mine, 12x12: a round black body with spikes north, south, east,
         west and on the four diagonals, and a white highlight up and to the
         left — the shape everyone recognises. Built as rows of characters so
         it can be read and edited as a picture in the Browser."
        | a |
        a := OrderedCollection new.
        a add: '.....KK.....'.
        a add: '.....KK.....'.
        a add: '..K..KK..K..'.
        a add: '...KKKKKK...'.
        a add: '..KWWKKKKK..'.
        a add: 'KKKWWKKKKKKK'.
        a add: 'KKKKKKKKKKKK'.
        a add: '..KKKKKKKK..'.
        a add: '...KKKKKK...'.
        a add: '..K..KK..K..'.
        a add: '.....KK.....'.
        a add: '.....KK.....'.
        ^a asArray
    ]

    flagArt [
        "The flag, 12x12: a red pennant on a black staff standing on a base."
        | a |
        a := OrderedCollection new.
        a add: '......K.....'.
        a add: '....RRK.....'.
        a add: '..RRRRK.....'.
        a add: 'RRRRRRK.....'.
        a add: '..RRRRK.....'.
        a add: '....RRK.....'.
        a add: '......K.....'.
        a add: '......K.....'.
        a add: '......K.....'.
        a add: '....KKKKK...'.
        a add: '...KKKKKKK..'.
        a add: '............'.
        ^a asArray
    ]

    "--- the numbers, drawn INTO the scrolling buffer ---
     Not with the pane's text overlay, which was the obvious first choice and
     the wrong one. That overlay lives in VIEWPORT coordinates and does not
     scroll, so the moment the explosion shakes the view every number would
     stay nailed to the glass while its square slid out from under it. Drawing
     them into the same buffer as the board means the whole picture moves
     together, for free, and costs no per-number command at all."

    drawNumber: n atX: x y: y [
        "One digit, centred in its square: a 5x7 glyph doubled is 10x14 in a
         16-pixel cell, so 3 across and 1 down centres it."
        self stampGlyph: (self digitArt: n)
             at: x0 + (x * cell) + 3
             y: y0 + (y * cell) + 1
             color: Minesweeper cNumber + n
    ]

    stampGlyph: rows at: px y: py color: c [
        "Draw a 5x7 glyph at double size — every set pixel becomes a 2x2 block,
         which is what makes a 5x7 font legible at this cell size."
        | row |
        1 to: rows size do: [ :r |
            row := rows at: r.
            1 to: row size do: [ :cIdx |
                "`=` not `==` — see colourFor:."
                (row at: cIdx) = $# ifTrue: [
                    self fill: px + ((cIdx - 1) * 2)
                         y: py + ((r - 1) * 2)
                         width: 2 height: 2 color: c ] ] ].
        ^self
    ]

    digitArt: n [
        "The counts 1..8 as 5x7 pixel art — the same shapes the pane's own font
         uses, kept here so the numbers can live in the scrolling buffer."
        | a |
        a := OrderedCollection new.
        n = 1 ifTrue: [
            a add: '..#..'. a add: '.##..'. a add: '..#..'. a add: '..#..'.
            a add: '..#..'. a add: '..#..'. a add: '.###.'. ^a asArray ].
        n = 2 ifTrue: [
            a add: '.###.'. a add: '#...#'. a add: '....#'. a add: '...#.'.
            a add: '..#..'. a add: '.#...'. a add: '#####'. ^a asArray ].
        n = 3 ifTrue: [
            a add: '.###.'. a add: '#...#'. a add: '....#'. a add: '..##.'.
            a add: '....#'. a add: '#...#'. a add: '.###.'. ^a asArray ].
        n = 4 ifTrue: [
            a add: '...#.'. a add: '..##.'. a add: '.#.#.'. a add: '#..#.'.
            a add: '#####'. a add: '...#.'. a add: '...#.'. ^a asArray ].
        n = 5 ifTrue: [
            a add: '#####'. a add: '#....'. a add: '####.'. a add: '....#'.
            a add: '....#'. a add: '#...#'. a add: '.###.'. ^a asArray ].
        n = 6 ifTrue: [
            a add: '..##.'. a add: '.#...'. a add: '#....'. a add: '####.'.
            a add: '#...#'. a add: '#...#'. a add: '.###.'. ^a asArray ].
        n = 7 ifTrue: [
            a add: '#####'. a add: '....#'. a add: '...#.'. a add: '..#..'.
            a add: '.#...'. a add: '.#...'. a add: '.#...'. ^a asArray ].
        a add: '.###.'. a add: '#...#'. a add: '#...#'. a add: '.###.'.
        a add: '#...#'. a add: '#...#'. a add: '.###.'. ^a asArray
    ]

    drawHud [
        "Mines remaining on the left, the clock on the right, and whatever the
         game has to say between them — now on the TEXT PLANE
         (docs/shared_screen_memory_design.md SM1) rather than the command-
         based overlay this used to send.

         The difference is the whole slice. Every string here USED to be a
         command, so redrawing the HUD had a cost and the code had to earn it
         by proving something had changed — and the first version of this
         method forgot to, which is how it came to re-send a command per
         revealed number sixty times a second. These are stores into a shared
         cell grid. Rewriting the whole HUD every frame is now cheaper than
         deciding whether to, and that class of bug cannot happen here at all.

         Still viewport-fixed, so it holds still while the explosion shakes
         the board: the text plane does not scroll with the picture, which is
         exactly what this HUD wanted."
        | status ink |
        pane textClearPlane.
        pane textPut: 'MINES ', self minesLeft printString
             at: 1 row: 0 color: GamePane white.
        pane textPut: 'TIME ', self elapsedSeconds printString
             at: 40 row: 0 color: GamePane white.
        over ifTrue: [
            won
                ifTrue: [ status := 'CLEARED'. ink := GamePane green ]
                ifFalse: [ status := 'BOOM'. ink := GamePane red ].
            pane textPut: status at: 22 row: 0 color: ink ].
        pane textPut: 'LEFT REVEAL  RIGHT FLAG  SPACE NEW  ESC QUIT'
             at: 1 row: 29 color: GamePane lightGrey.
        ^self
    ]
]
